, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}J \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{1}{9}I \\\\ \\frac{10}{9}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi /3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}))}{10}+\\frac{{e}^{-0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0))}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi /3}}{10}+\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}({e}^{-\\pi /3}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429379 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429379 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 424->1717. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429379 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186049543 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429380 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429380 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossin